.docname {Linting & Formatting}
.include {docs}

.glossary
    | Word | Meaning | Example |
    |---|---|---|
    | Linter | A tool that reads code and reports likely mistakes, without running it | `clippy` warns about a needless `clone()` |
    | Formatter | A tool that rewrites spacing and line breaks into one fixed style | `gofmt -w .` |
    | Type checker | A tool that checks types in a language that does not force them | `pyright`, `vue-tsc` |
    | Task | A named command moon knows how to run for a project | `telemetry:lint` |
    | Affected | A project whose files changed on the current branch | Only `telemetry` after a change in `backend/telemetry/src` |
    | Cache hit | moon skips a task because its inputs did not change since the last pass | `telemetry:lint` in 0 s |
    | Flat config | ESLint's config format: one exported list of rule blocks | `tooling/eslint-config/index.mjs` |

Every language has a linter and a formatter. `just lint` runs them through moon; CI runs them through one workflow template per language. This page says what runs, where it is configured, and where local and CI differ.

---

## What runs, per language

| Language | Projects | Lint (`just lint`) | Fix (`just lint fix`) | CI template |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| Rust | 6 services, 4 `*-schema` crates | `cargo clippy -- -D warnings` | nothing | `tpl-rust-ci.yml`: `cargo fmt --check`, then `cargo clippy --release --all-targets -- -D warnings` |
| Go | 4 services, 3 `backend/libs` modules | `gofmt -l .` must print nothing, then `go vet ./...` | nothing | `tpl-go-ci.yml`: same two checks |
| Python, `agent` | `backend/agent` | `npm run lint`: `ruff check`, `ruff format --check`, `pyright` | `ruff check --fix`, `ruff format` | `tpl-python-ci.yml`: same three |
| Python, collector | `devices/ap-collector` | `uv run` of `ruff check`, `ruff format --check`, `pyright` | `ruff check --fix`, `ruff format` | `tpl-python-ci.yml` |
| Python, simulators | `ap-simulator`, `aq-simulator` | `uv run ruff check . && uv run pyright`, **no format check** | `ruff check --fix`, `ruff format` | `ci-simulators.yml`: same two |
| TypeScript, frontend | `frontend` | `eslint .` | `eslint . --fix --cache` | `ci-frontend.yml`: `npm run lint`, `npm run type-check` |
| TypeScript, Node | `sensor-simulator` | `eslint .` | `prettier --write`, then `eslint . --fix` | `ci-simulators.yml` |

- Tool versions: Rust `1.99.1` and Go `1.27.3` are pinned in `.mise.toml`, and CI reads the same pins, so `clippy` and `go vet` report the same warnings on both sides. Python tools come from each `uv.lock`, ESLint from each `package-lock.json`.
- `backend/acceptance` has no lint task: its `moon.yml` excludes the inherited `lint` and `lint-fix`.

---

## `just lint affected`, end to end

.mermaid
    sequenceDiagram
        autonumber
        participant D as Developer
        participant J as just<br/>just/lint.just
        participant W as check-workflow-registry.py
        participant M as moon
        participant P as one project task
        D->>J: just lint affected
        J->>W: recipe workflows runs first
        W-->>J: services.json and workflows agree
        J->>M: mise exec -- moon run :lint --affected
        M->>M: git diff against the base branch
        M->>M: keep projects whose files changed
        loop each affected project
            M->>M: hash the task inputs
            alt same hash as last pass
                M-->>D: cache hit, skipped
            else inputs changed
                M->>P: run lint in the project folder
                P-->>M: exit code
            end
        end
        M-->>D: summary, non-zero if one failed

| Step | What happens | Real value |
|---|---|---|
| 1 | `lint.just` declares `affected: workflows`, so `workflows` runs first | |
| 2 | Runs with CI's pinned `pyyaml` from `.github/requirements-lint.txt` | `uv run --no-project --with-requirements …` |
| 3 | A workflow with a dangling `needs:` fails here, not in a 0 s GitHub run with no logs | |
| 4 | `mise exec` puts the pinned tools on `PATH` | moon `2.6.4` |
| 5–6 | Only projects with a changed file stay | change in `backend/telemetry/src/kernel/ingest.rs` keeps `telemetry` |
| 7 | Inputs for a Rust lint: `src/**/*`, `Cargo.toml` | from `.moon/tasks/rust.yml` |
| 9 | Rust project | `cargo clippy -- -D warnings` in `backend/telemetry` |

**Example.** A branch changes one file in `backend/telemetry` and one in `frontend/src`. moon runs `telemetry:lint` and `frontend:lint`. No other project runs. Run again with no change: both are cache hits.

.mermaid
    flowchart TD
        A["just lint all or affected"] --> B{"workflows check passes?"}
        B -- no --> X["stop, list the dangling needs or uses"]
        B -- yes --> C{"affected only?"}
        C -- yes --> D["projects with changed files"]
        C -- no --> E["every project"]
        D --> F{"inputs hash seen before?"}
        E --> F
        F -- yes --> G["skip, cache hit"]
        F -- no --> H["run the task"]

`just lint fix` runs `moon run :lint-fix` with caching off: a fix rewrites files, so a cached "nothing to do" would be wrong.

---

## Rust: `clippy` and `rustfmt`

- No `clippy.toml` or `rustfmt.toml` in the repo: both run with their defaults.
- `-D warnings` turns every warning into an error. A warning cannot pile up unseen.

| | Local `moon run :lint` | CI `tpl-rust-ci.yml` |
|---|---|---|
| Formatting | **not checked** | `cargo fmt --check` |
| Targets | library and binaries | `--all-targets`: tests and examples too |
| Profile | debug | `--release` |

**Trap.** `just lint` passes on code that CI refuses for two reasons:

1. A file `rustfmt` would change. Run `cargo fmt` in the crate before pushing.
2. A clippy warning inside `tests/`. Run `cargo clippy --all-targets -- -D warnings` to see it.

`just lint fix` does nothing for Rust: no Rust project defines `lint-fix`.

---

## Go: `gofmt` and `go vet`

The lint task, from `.moon/tasks/go.yml`:

1. `gofmt -l .` lists unformatted files. It exits `0` even when it lists some, so the task wraps it in `test -z`: a non-empty list fails.
2. On failure it prints the files and the fix, `gofmt -w .`.
3. Then `go vet ./...`, which finds suspicious code: a wrong `Printf` verb, an unreachable line, a copied lock.

`tpl-go-ci.yml` runs the same two checks, and prints each unformatted file as a GitHub annotation on the file. `just lint fix` does nothing for Go: no Go project defines `lint-fix`.

---

## Python: `ruff` and `pyright`

Config lives in each `pyproject.toml`. For `agent`:

| Setting | Value | Why |
|---|---|---|
| `line-length` | 100 | |
| Rule families | `E W F I B UP N S ASYNC SIM PTH TID TCH RUF C4 PIE RET` | Errors, imports order, likely bugs, security (`S`), async misuse |
| Ignored | `E501`, `B008`, `S101` | The formatter owns line length; FastAPI `Depends()` in defaults; `assert` |
| Per folder | `tests/**` and `evals/**` skip `S`; `alembic/**` skips `N` | Test fixtures look like secrets; migration names are generated |
| `ruff format` | double quotes, spaces | |
| `pyright` | `basic` mode, on `app` and `tests` | A missing import is an error; an unused one is a warning |

**Trap.** The inherited Python `lint` task calls `npm run lint`. Only `agent` has a `package.json` wrapping `uv`; the root `.gitignore` ignores every other `package.json`. So the simulators and the collector override `lint` to call `uv` directly, and `backend/acceptance` turns it off. A new Python project that does neither fails with "missing script".

---

## TypeScript: ESLint, Prettier, `vue-tsc`

Two configs:

| Config | Used by | Content |
|---|---|---|
| `tooling/eslint-config/index.mjs`, package `@crowdvision/eslint-config` | `sensor-simulator`, via a one-line `eslint.config.mjs` | `@eslint/js` recommended, `typescript-eslint` recommended, Node and Jest globals, `eslint-config-prettier` last |
| `frontend/eslint.config.ts` | `frontend` | `eslint-plugin-vue` `flat/essential`, Vue TypeScript recommended, Vitest rules on `src/**/__tests__/*`, Playwright rules on `e2e/**`, `skip-formatting` last |

- Both turn off `no-explicit-any` and `no-unused-vars`.
- The Prettier config comes **last** in both, so ESLint never reports a spacing problem that Prettier owns.
- `vue-tsc --build` checks types in `.vue` files. It runs in `npm run build` and in CI's `type-check` step, not in `just lint`.
- moon's TypeScript `lint` lists `/tooling/eslint-config/index.mjs` as an input, so changing the shared config re-lints `sensor-simulator`. `frontend` replaces its inputs with `src/**/*` and `eslint.config.ts`.

---

## Workflows

`just lint workflows` runs `scripts/check-workflow-registry.py`. It checks `.github/workflows/*.yml` against `.github/services.json`, and reports any `needs:` or `uses:` that points at nothing. `ci-gate.yml` runs the same script in its `workflows-lint` job.

---

## Choices

| Choice | Why |
|---|---|
| One pin per tool, in `.mise.toml` | A new compiler warns about new things; local and CI must agree |
| `-D warnings` | A warning ignored once is ignored forever |
| Lint through moon | Only changed projects run, and an unchanged one is free |
| Formatter config last in ESLint | Two tools never argue about the same space |

See also: [Build System](build-system.qd), [CI](ci.qd).
